{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YbDgfXxtxnrmqSxHMZQAYU","type":"tool_result","content":"3\n743:Chapter 10 drew you two axes and filled in one of them. NetworkPolicy governs which Pod may open a connection to which Pod: reachability, workload to workload. The other axis, it said, was what a Pod may do to the machine it is running on, and it was Chapter 12's. Chapter 11 pointed at the same place twice: once when it warned you about `hostPath` and called this *the workload-to-host boundary problem, and why an entire security apparatus exists to police it*, and once when it insisted that a volume access mode is not a permission system.\n885:   hostPath volumes               allowed      ✗ forbidden ✗ forbidden\n930:The rows in that figure are the documented Baseline and Restricted controls: Baseline forbids `privileged`, host namespaces (`hostNetwork`, `hostPID`, `hostIPC`), `hostPath` volumes, and unconfined seccomp; restricts `hostPort`, added capabilities to a known list, AppArmor and SELinux to approved values, `/proc` mount type to `Default`, and sysctls to a safe list. Restricted adds every Baseline requirement plus: volumes limited to a safe list; `allowPrivilegeEscalation: false`; `runAsNonRoot: true`; `runAsUser` non-zero or unset; seccomp explicitly `RuntimeDefault` or `Localhost`; and capabilities dropping `ALL` with only `NET_BIND_SERVICE` addable back [source: k8s-docs-pod-security-standards-profiles-2026-08-31].","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9b2449d8-8450-4582-a242-42c21f189a2a","uuid":"ce1f6d06-e0f9-4312-962f-c9cee182699f","timestamp":"2026-08-31T05:13:21.214Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"3\n743:Chapter 10 drew you two axes and filled in one of them. NetworkPolicy governs which Pod may open a connection to which Pod: reachability, workload to workload. The other axis, it said, was what a Pod may do to the machine it is running on, and it was Chapter 12's. Chapter 11 pointed at the same place twice: once when it warned you about `hostPath` and called this *the workload-to-host boundary problem, and why an entire security apparatus exists to police it*, and once when it insisted that a volume access mode is not a permission system.\n885:   hostPath volumes               allowed      ✗ forbidden ✗ forbidden\n930:The rows in that figure are the documented Baseline and Restricted controls: Baseline forbids `privileged`, host namespaces (`hostNetwork`, `hostPID`, `hostIPC`), `hostPath` volumes, and unconfined seccomp; restricts `hostPort`, added capabilities to a known list, AppArmor and SELinux to approved values, `/proc` mount type to `Default`, and sysctls to a safe list. Restricted adds every Baseline requirement plus: volumes limited to a safe list; `allowPrivilegeEscalation: false`; `runAsNonRoot: true`; `runAsUser` non-zero or unset; seccomp explicitly `RuntimeDefault` or `Localhost`; and capabilities dropping `ALL` with only `NET_BIND_SERVICE` addable back [source: k8s-docs-pod-security-standards-profiles-2026-08-31].","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
