{"duration_api_ms":376602,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"9b2449d8-8450-4582-a242-42c21f189a2a","total_cost_usd":4.794688999999999,"usage":{"input_tokens":38,"cache_creation_input_tokens":222578,"cache_read_input_tokens":3757488,"output_tokens":27599,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":14097},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":222578,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1746,"cache_read_input_tokens":229578,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11592,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11592},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":38,"outputTokens":27599,"cacheReadInputTokens":3757488,"cacheCreationInputTokens":222578,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":4.794688999999999,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[{"tool_name":"Bash","tool_use_id":"toolu_01UjVXH9q5YuKbEhjrvboVVt","tool_input":{"command":"cd \"/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/.pipeline-state/ch-12\" && python -c \"\nimport re,sys\nsys.stdout.reconfigure(encoding='utf-8')\nt=open('draft-v1.md',encoding='utf-8').read()\nlines=t.split('\\n')\nmarks=[(i,l) for i,l in enumerate(lines) if re.match(r'^## ',l)]\nmarks.append((len(lines),'EOF'))\ntot=len(t.split())\nprint('TOTAL WORDS:',tot)\nprint()\nfor (a,la),(b,_) in zip(marks,marks[1:]):\n    w=len(' '.join(lines[a:b]).split())\n    name=la[3:60]\n    print(f'{w:6d}  {100*w/tot:5.1f}%  {name}')\n\"","description":"Word count per section of the draft"}},{"tool_name":"Bash","tool_use_id":"toolu_01TfnMMfu4a3EHe17WXxget8","tool_input":{"command":"python -c \"\nimport re,sys\nsys.stdout.reconfigure(encoding='utf-8')\np=r'C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\ch-12\\draft-v1.md'\nt=open(p,encoding='utf-8').read()\nlines=t.split('\\n')\nmarks=[(i,l) for i,l in enumerate(lines) if re.match(r'^## ',l)]\nmarks.append((len(lines),'EOF'))\ntot=len(t.split())\nprint('TOTAL WORDS:',tot)\nfor (a,la),(b,_) in zip(marks,marks[1:]):\n    w=len(' '.join(lines[a:b]).split())\n    print(f'{w:6d}  {100*w/tot:5.1f}%  {la[3:64]}')\n\"","description":"Compute per-section word counts in draft"}},{"tool_name":"Bash","tool_use_id":"toolu_015sdMK4zqfEfkVJubq2V1ay","tool_input":{"command":"awk '/^## /{if(n)printf \"%6d  %s\\n\",w,n; n=substr($0,4,60); w=0} {w+=NF} END{if(n)printf \"%6d  %s\\n\",w,n} ' \"/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/.pipeline-state/ch-12/draft-v1.md\"; echo \"--- TOTAL ---\"; wc -w < \"/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/.pipeline-state/ch-12/draft-v1.md\"","description":"Per-section word counts via awk"}},{"tool_name":"Bash","tool_use_id":"toolu_01EMuXL5ZYQxLB2C1fXDPqQ2","tool_input":{"command":"awk '/^## /{if(n)printf \"%6d  %s\\n\",w,n; n=substr($0,4,60); w=0} {w+=NF} END{if(n)printf \"%6d  %s\\n\",w,n}' \"/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/.pipeline-state/ch-12/draft-v1.md\"","description":"Per-section word counts via awk"}},{"tool_name":"Write","tool_use_id":"toolu_01UiTC76Y4c6Na83of3wtjsg","tool_input":{"file_path":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\ch-12\\diagnostics\\curriculum-alignment.md","content":"# Curriculum-Alignment Audit — KCNA Chapter 12\n\n**Stage 7 · Locks, Keys, and Watchstanders · audited 2026-08-31**\nDraft audited: `.pipeline-state/ch-12/draft-v1.md` (1,776 lines). Line references are to that file.\nAuthority: `sources/cncf-kcna-curriculum-pdf-2026-08-23.md`, corroborated by\n`cncf-kcna-certification-page-2026-08-23.md` and `lf-kcna-program-changes-2026-08-23.md`.\n\n---\n\n## Reading this audit: what the authority actually publishes\n\nThe template for this stage assumes numbered objective IDs (`1.1`, `2.3`). **The KCNA curriculum\ndoes not have them.** The CNCF publication is three pages long and stops at the competency level:\n\n> - 44% – Kubernetes Fundamentals: Kubernetes Core Concepts; Administration; Scheduling; Containerization\n> - **28% – Container Orchestration: Networking; Security; Troubleshooting; Storage**\n> - 16% – Cloud Native Application Delivery: Application Delivery; Debugging\n> - 12% – Cloud Native Architecture: Observability; Cloud Native Ecosystem and Principles; Cloud Native Community and Collaboration\n\nThat is the entire objective inventory. There are no sub-objectives, no per-competency weights, and\nno enumerated learning outcomes. So:\n\n- **`D2.2` is a book-internal ID**, not a CNCF one — Domain 2, competency 2 (Security), counting the\n  competencies in the order the curriculum lists them. It is used consistently across Chapters 1–11\n  and it resolves correctly. No drift there.\n- **At authority grain, this chapter has exactly one objective**, and the answer to \"is it covered\"\n  is an unqualified yes. Everything finer than that is the book's own decomposition.\n\nThe audit therefore runs at two grains: the authority row, then the outline's own concept-level\ndecomposition of D2.2, which is the grain at which real gaps live. Rows below the authority line are\nmarked *(book-internal)* so no downstream stage mistakes them for published objectives.\n\n---\n\n## Objectives the outline claims to cover\n\n| Objective ID | Description | Covered in draft? | Depth |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **D2** | Container Orchestration — 28% *(published)* | YES | appropriate — chapter claims only the Security competency |\n| **D2.2** | Security *(competency name published; ID book-internal)* | YES | appropriate |\n| — *(book-internal decomposition of D2.2 below)* | | | |\n| D2.2-a | Cloud native security lifecycle phases (develop / distribute / deploy / runtime), incl. runtime's access / compute / storage split | YES — §1, L143–L195 | appropriate |\n| D2.2-b | The 4Cs layer framing (Cloud / Cluster / Container / Code) | YES — §1, L197–L211 | appropriate |\n| D2.2-c | RBAC: four objects, the derivation, rules, aggregation, additive semantics, binding immutability, default roles | YES — §3, L344–L550 | deep — correctly the chapter's centre of gravity |\n| D2.2-d | ServiceAccounts, the `default` account, TokenRequest, projected tokens | YES — §2, L237–L342 | appropriate |\n| D2.2-e | ServiceAccount **token hardening** | **partial** — §2 covers TokenRequest-over-Secret; `automountServiceAccountToken: false` absent | shallow — see Fix 3 |\n| D2.2-f | Secret types, the three exposure paths, hardening list | YES — §4, L620–L739 | appropriate |\n| D2.2-g | Encryption at rest, `EncryptionConfiguration` | YES — §4, L690–L706 | appropriate |\n| D2.2-h | `securityContext`: scopes, fields, capabilities, seccomp/AppArmor, privileged | YES — §5, L743–L842 | appropriate |\n| D2.2-i | Pod Security Standards — three levels, cumulative | YES — §6, L856–L935 | appropriate |\n| D2.2-j | Pod Security Admission — three modes, namespace labels | **partial** — exemptions and the enforce/workload-resource asymmetry absent | shallow — see Fix 2 |\n| D2.2-k | Supply chain: scanning, signing, SBOM, in-toto, TUF, Notary, Harbor | YES — §7, L1057–L1186 | **deep — over-covered**, see Depth |\n| D2.2-l | Image scanning as a practice, and what a CVE is | **partial** — one sentence, **unsourced** (L1096) | shallow — see Fix 4 |\n| D2.2-m | Policy engines: OPA/Gatekeeper/Rego, Kyverno, Falco; admission vs runtime | YES — §8, L1190–L1257 | appropriate |\n| D2.2-n | Sandboxed runtimes as a security control | YES by reference — §5, L826 → Ch 2 §7 | appropriate — deliberate non-re-teach, correct |\n\n**Nothing the outline claimed is missing outright.** The three partials (e, j, l) are all cases where\na fetched snapshot contains material the draft did not use, which makes them cheap to close.\n\n---\n\n## Objectives covered in the draft but NOT in the outline\n\nThe outline declares `kb_tags.objectives: [\"D2.2\"]` and nothing else, for all nine sections. The draft\nmaterially teaches from snapshots that the research stage tagged to **two other domains**:\n\n| Domain | Where it enters the draft | Snapshots involved | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **D4 — Cloud Native Ecosystem and Principles** (12%) | §1's lifecycle phases; §7's CNCF project roster (in-toto, TUF, Harbor, Notary); §8's policy-as-code framing and Falco | `k8s-docs-cloud-native-security`, `in-toto-overview`, `tuf-overview`, `harbor-overview`, `notary-project-signing-digest`, `cncf-glossary-policy-as-code`, `falco-overview` — all carry `D4` in `objectives_covered` | **Under-declaration, not scope creep.** Legitimate and worth keeping. Record it. |\n| **D1 — Core Concepts / Containerization** (44%) | §2's owner-reference derivation; §2/§4's ServiceAccount and Secret object material; §7's Sigstore artifact framing | `k8s-docs-garbage-collection` (`D1.1`), `k8s-docs-service-accounts` and `k8s-docs-secret` (secondary `D1 Core Concepts`), `sigstore-overview` (secondary `D1 Containerization`) | **Retrieval, not new teaching.** Correctly handled as cross-bearings. Record as secondary only. |\n\nThis matters beyond bookkeeping: **D4 is 12% of the exam and this is one of the few chapters that\nteaches its ecosystem content at any depth.** If `kb_tags` continues to declare D2.2 exclusively, the\nbook-level coverage rollup — and Ch 19 §2's confusion matrix, which draws on it — will under-count\nD4 and may trigger a phantom \"D4 under-covered\" finding at book close.\n\n### Genuine scope creep (material above associate tier)\n\nThree items exceed what the authority's competency names can justify, all in §7 and §5:\n\n1. **Standards-body identifiers** — `ISO/IEC 5962:2021` and `ECMA-424` (L1151–L1153). A KCNA\n   candidate is tested on recognising SPDX and CycloneDX as the two BOM standards, not on their\n   ISO/Ecma numbers.\n2. **TUF's four attack scenarios, quoted in full** (L1163–L1168). Four verbatim bullets for a project\n   the chapter's own difficulty rating (🟡, recognition depth) says is tested by name.\n3. **AppArmor-vs-SELinux policy-application mechanics** — \"In AppArmor, you define resources using\n   file paths. SELinux uses the index node (inode) of a resource\" (L807). This is CKS-tier detail.\n   The *existence* of both modules and what each does is D2.2; the inode/path distinction is not.\n\nCounter-example worth recording, because the draft got this right: §4 names `EncryptionConfiguration`\nand the `identity` provider trap but **declines to enumerate the six providers** (aescbc, aesgcm, kms\nv1/v2, secretbox) that the `k8s-docs-encrypt-data` snapshot offers. That is exactly the correct call\nat this tier, and it is the model the three items above should follow.\n\n---\n\n## Depth mismatches\n\nThe authority publishes no sub-competency weight, so per-objective weight cannot be computed —\ncorrectly disclosed by the draft at L6–L8. The chapter's ~7% allocation is `28% ÷ 4 competencies`,\nwhich is defensible and transparently labelled.\n\nDepth *within* the chapter is therefore judged against the two evidence sources that do exist:\n**B1's ten sourced D2.2 traps**, and the book's own assessment allocation. Prose share is measured in\nlines of teaching body (1,066 lines across the nine sections; checkpoints, Exam Alert and Practice\nQuestions excluded).\n\n| Section | Prose share | Practice Qs | Sourced traps | Mismatch |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| §1 Layers and phases | 8.8% | 2 | 0 | OK |\n| §2 Identity | 10.2% | 2 | 1 (#62) | OK |\n| §3 RBAC | 19.4% | 5 | **7** (#53–#59) | OK — largest section, largest trap cluster |\n| §4 Secrets | 11.5% | 3 | 2 (#60, #61) | OK — see note |\n| §5 `securityContext` | 9.7% | 3 | 0 | OK |\n| §6 PSS / PSA | 12.2% | 2 (+1 interleaved) | 0 | OK |\n| §7 Supply chain | **12.5%** | 2 | **0** | **over-covered** |\n| §8 Policy engines | 6.7% | 1 (+1 interleaved) | 0 | OK — the model for §7 |\n| §9 Zenith | 9.0% | 1 | — | OK — synthesis, tested by interleaving |\n\n**§7 is the one real depth mismatch, and it is over-coverage.** It is the second-largest section in\nthe chapter, and it carries:\n\n- zero sourced traps in B1's 114-item inventory;\n- the chapter's own 🟡 rating, assigned precisely *because* the outline read that zero as evidence of\n  recognition-depth testing;\n- two of the twenty-one Practice Questions;\n- **and the chapter's two unclosed research gaps** (G-A, G-B).\n\nSo the section with the least evidence of being tested is consuming the second-most prose, and it is\ndoing so partly on material that could not be sourced. §8 covers comparably ecosystem-facing content\n— three tools, two enforcement positions — in **half the space**, and does it better.\n\nThe fix is not to gut §7; its Fixed Point (a signature binds to a digest) is the chapter's second-best\nretrieval payoff and must stay at full strength. The fix is to compress the *roster*: the three\nscope-creep items above, plus the Sigstore component list, are ~15–20 lines that can go without\ntouching the checkpoint chain or the Fixed Point.\n\n**Note on §4.** The outline allocated 4 Practice Questions to §4 and the draft delivered 3, moving one\nto §5. §4 holds the chapter's designated highest-consequence fact and 2 of 10 sourced traps; §5 holds\nnone. This is mild and partly offset by Checkpoint #2's challenge item being a §4 scenario, so it is\nrecorded rather than flagged — but if any question is reallocated during revision, this is the one.\n\n**Nothing is under-covered on the depth axis.** The three shallow rows in the objectives table are\ncompleteness gaps, not proportion gaps.\n\n---\n\n## Gaps the research stage flagged\n\n`research-manifest.md` closed G5, G6, G7, G22 (mostly) and G23, and opened eight residual gaps.\nVerdict on each, against the draft:\n\n| Gap | Manifest instruction | Draft handling | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **G-A** CVE has no primary-source definition | May state the CVE Program mission sentence, attributed. **May not** define \"vulnerability\" | L1096 defines CVE as \"an identifier for a publicly disclosed vulnerability\" — **no source tag, no `[inferred]` marker**. The `AUTHOR-REVIEW` at L1100 covers scanning-as-practice, not this sentence | **NOT handled** |\n| **G-B** No one-sentence SBOM definition | Draft around it; do not quote a definition | L1147–L1153 builds from what SPDX/CycloneDX say their standards cover; `AUTHOR-REVIEW` at L1157 flags it precisely | **Handled well** |\n| **G-C** File-over-env-var second half unsourced | Honour `chapter-11:444` with the sourced half only | L708–L724 supplies update-propagation + per-container scoping, then **explicitly declines** the leak claim in prose; `AUTHOR-REVIEW` at L714 | **Handled well** — best gap handling in the chapter |\n| **G-D** Why RBAC names subjects | Must carry an uncertainty signal, not the sourced register | L482 `AUTHOR-REVIEW`; prose at L484 says \"the documentation does not answer it, so here is the reading that makes sense of it\" | **Handled** |\n| **G-E** §9's design rationale unsourceable | Deliver as the author's reading | L1358–L1362 states the situation *before* the argument: \"The documentation states the property and does not explain it… a reading, not a citation\" | **Handled well** |\n| **G-F** Orphaned identity after deletion | Draft as `[inferred]` **or** derive from garbage collection with a Ch 6 pointer | L330–L336 takes the second option: cites `k8s-docs-garbage-collection` for owner references, then derives, with the Ch 6 §3 cross-bearing | **Handled** (second option) |\n| **G-G** Cosign tag→digest behaviour | Attribute to Notary Project, not Sigstore | L1124–L1128 and PQ19 both attribute to `notary-project-signing-digest`. No misattribution to Sigstore anywhere | **Handled** |\n| **G-H** PodSecurityPolicy removal implied, not stated | Fetch the migration page, **or** soften to \"superseded\" | L962 states \"**it was removed in Kubernetes 1.25**\" as bare fact, no tag. Repeated in the Exam Alert table (L1454) and the §6 heading block. No fetch was performed | **NOT handled — and hardened** |\n\n**Six of eight handled, two not.** G-H is the more serious of the two: the manifest gave an explicit\ntwo-option instruction, the draft took neither, and it added a version number more specific than any\nsnapshot in the 184-file corpus supports. The claim is very likely true in the world — but this book's\nwhole sourcing discipline is that \"true\" and \"sourced\" are tracked separately, and B1 carries this as\na `[source]` trap that the corpus cannot currently back.\n\nPer Rule 3, neither of these is a curriculum-alignment failure — objective D2.2 is still covered.\nThey are sourcing defects inside covered material, recorded here because this stage is where the\nresearch manifest and the draft are read against each other.\n\n---\n\n## Additional finding: dangling source tag on the metadata line\n\nNot a coverage issue, but it surfaced while resolving the authority and it is a one-line fix.\n\n**L4** reads:\n\n```\n**Domain: Container Orchestration (D2) — 28% of the exam** [source: cncf-kcna-curriculum-2025-11-24] ...\n```\n\n`cncf-kcna-curriculum-2025-11-24` **matches no file in `sources/`** (184 snapshots checked). `2025-11-24`\nis the blueprint's *effective date*, taken from `lf-kcna-program-changes`; it is not a snapshot name.\nEvery other one of the draft's 26 distinct source tags resolves correctly — this is the only dangling one.\n\nAll eleven shipped chapters use `cncf-kcna-curriculum-pdf-2026-08-23`. Ch 10 L203 is the house form:\n\n```\n**Domain: Container Orchestration (competency: Networking) | Domain weight: 28% [source: cncf-kcna-curriculum-pdf-2026-08-23] | ...**\n```\n\nNote the draft also diverges from house form on the competency clause and the \"Domain weight:\" label.\n\n---\n\n## Recommended fixes\n\nOrdered by cost-to-benefit. Fixes 1, 2 and 4 are the ones that should not ship without action.\n\n**Fix 1 — repair the metadata line (§ metadata, L4).**\nReplace `[source: cncf-kcna-curriculum-2025-11-24]` with `[source: cncf-kcna-curriculum-pdf-2026-08-23]`\nand conform the line to the Ch 9/10/11 house form, naming the competency:\n`**Domain: Container Orchestration (competency: Security) | Domain weight: 28% [source: cncf-kcna-curriculum-pdf-2026-08-23] | Chapter allocation: ~7% | Complexity: Mixed | Novelty: Moderate**`.\nThe ~7% disclaimer block at L6–L8 is excellent and stays as written.\n\n**Fix 2 — close G-H, and close D2.2-j at the same time (§6, L958–L962 + L1454).**\nTwo options, and they can both be taken:\n- *Soften the removal claim.* Replace \"it was removed in Kubernetes 1.25\" with \"it is superseded by\n  Pod Security Admission\" — supported by `k8s-docs-pod-security-standards-profiles`'s Policy\n  Instantiation section and by the PSA page's migration pointer. Mirror in the Exam Alert row (L1454),\n  which currently reads \"Removed in 1.25\". If the specific version is wanted, fetch\n  `kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/migrate-from-psp/` first.\n- *Add the unused PSA material.* `k8s-docs-pod-security-admission-2026-08-31` contains, unused:\n  **\"both the audit and warning modes are applied to the workload resources. However, enforce mode is\n  not applied to workload resources, only to the resulting pod objects.\"** That is a sourced,\n  exam-shaped discrimination fact, and it sharpens §6's own Ch 13 plant at L966–L970: the Deployment\n  is accepted, and its Pods are rejected — which is precisely the failure shape with \"no phase, no\n  events, and no logs\" that The Voyage Ahead promises. Two sentences. Exemptions (usernames,\n  RuntimeClassNames, namespaces) can be one further clause or omitted.\n\n**Fix 3 — close D2.2-e (§2, after the TokenRequest block ~L322).**\nAdd one sentence on `automountServiceAccountToken: false`, sourced from\n`k8s-docs-rbac-good-practices-2026-08-31`'s Hardening list: *\"Avoid the default auto-mounting of\nservice account tokens by setting `automountServiceAccountToken: false`… workloads which require\nservice account tokens can still mount them.\"* This is the missing half of the \"how to harden its\ntokens\" quarter of the pinned `chapter-05:779` promise, which §2 currently discharges only as\nTokenRequest-over-legacy-Secret. One sentence, already sourced, closes a pinned debt.\n\n**Fix 4 — close G-A / D2.2-l (§7, L1096).**\nEither attribute or mark. The manifest permits, from the CVE Program's archived site: *\"The mission of\nthe CVE™ Program is to identify, define, and catalog publicly disclosed cybersecurity\nvulnerabilities.\"* Use that with an attribution to the CVE Program, or leave the current sentence and\nmark it `[inferred]`. Do not leave a definitional claim untagged in a section that already carries two\n`AUTHOR-REVIEW` flags — the asymmetry reads as an oversight rather than a decision.\n\n**Fix 5 — trim §7 by 15–20 lines (over-coverage).**\nCut, in order of confidence: the ISO/IEC 5962:2021 and ECMA-424 identifiers (L1151–L1153); TUF's four\nquoted attack bullets, compressed to one sentence naming what the four have in common — which is the\npoint the draft already makes at L1170 and makes well; and the Sigstore component list trimmed from\nfour bullets to Cosign/Fulcio/Rekor inline, keeping Policy Controller for its §8 hand-off. **Do not\ntouch** the digest-binding Fixed Point (L1124–L1136), the checkpoint chain, or `ch12-fig05`.\n\n**Fix 6 — extend `kb_tags.objectives` (outline frontmatter).**\nChange `objectives: [\"D2.2\"]` to `objectives: [\"D2.2\"]` primary with\n`objectives_secondary: [\"D4 Cloud Native Ecosystem and Principles\", \"D1 Core Concepts\"]`, matching the\n`objectives_covered` fields on the snapshots §1, §7 and §8 actually cite. Book-level bookkeeping only —\nno prose change — but it prevents a false \"D4 under-covered\" finding at book close.\n\n**Fix 7 — trim the AppArmor/SELinux mechanics (§5, L807).**\nDrop the file-path-vs-inode sentence; keep the configuration distinction (profiles vs labels), which is\nthe part that supports the summary table and the §6 hand-off. One sentence.\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n**Alignment verdict: PASS with four fixes.**\n\nObjective D2.2 is covered thoroughly and, in §3, exceptionally — the derivation-not-table treatment\ndischarges a debt two shipped chapters made in near-identical words, and the ten sourced traps land\nwhere the trap density is. Nothing the outline promised is absent from the draft.\n\nThe findings are: one dangling source tag (Fix 1); two research gaps the manifest flagged and the\ndraft did not close, one of which was hardened into a more specific claim than any source supports\n(Fix 2, Fix 4); two small completeness gaps against snapshots that were fetched for this chapter and\nthen not used (Fix 2, Fix 3); one section over-covered relative to every available signal of how it is\ntested (Fix 5); and an under-declaration of D4/D1 in `kb_tags` that is worth correcting for the\nbook-level rollup (Fix 6).\n\nGap handling is otherwise a strength: six of eight residual gaps are handled, and G-C, G-E and G-B are\nhandled better than the manifest asked for — the draft states what it cannot source *before* making the\nargument, in prose the reader sees, rather than only in a comment the reader does not.\n"}}],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":24,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"The audit is complete, but the write to `Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\ch-12\\diagnostics\\curriculum-alignment.md` needs permission that wasn't granted in this non-interactive session. The document is composed and ready — approve the write (or tell me a different path) and I'll commit it verbatim. Here are the findings so nothing is lost:\n\n## What I verified against\n\nThe task template assumes numbered objective IDs. **The KCNA curriculum has none** — `sources/cncf-kcna-curriculum-pdf-2026-08-23.md` is three pages and stops at competency level (D2 Container Orchestration 28%: Networking; Security; Troubleshooting; Storage). `D2.2` is a book-internal ID that resolves correctly and is used consistently across Ch 1–11. So the audit runs at two grains: the authority row, then the outline's own decomposition, explicitly labelled book-internal.\n\n## Verdict: PASS with four fixes\n\n**Nothing the outline claimed is missing.** §3's derivation-not-table treatment is exceptional, and the ten sourced traps land where trap density is.\n\nFour findings that shouldn't ship unaddressed:\n\n1. **Dangling source tag, L4.** The metadata line cites `cncf-kcna-curriculum-2025-11-24`. That matches **no file in `sources/`** (184 checked) — `2025-11-24` is the blueprint's effective date, not a snapshot. All eleven shipped chapters use `cncf-kcna-curriculum-pdf-2026-08-23`. It's the only dangling tag among the draft's 26.\n\n2. **G-H not handled, and hardened.** The manifest gave an explicit two-option instruction (fetch the migration page, or soften to \"superseded\"). The draft took neither and states \"**removed in Kubernetes 1.25**\" as bare untagged fact at L962 and L1454 — more specific than the corpus supports. Fixing it also closes a completeness gap: the PSA snapshot's unused sentence that *enforce is not applied to workload resources, only to the resulting pod objects* sharpens §6's own Ch 13 plant, since it means the Deployment is accepted and its Pods are rejected.\n\n3. **G-A not handled.** L1096 defines CVE with no source tag and no `[inferred]` marker, in a section that already carries two `AUTHOR-REVIEW` flags — the asymmetry reads as oversight.\n\n4. **§7 is over-covered.** Second-largest section (12.5% of teaching body), **zero** sourced traps, 🟡-rated precisely because that zero was read as recognition-depth evidence, and it carries both unclosed research gaps. §8 does comparable ecosystem content in half the space. Trim the roster (ISO/IEC 5962, ECMA-424, TUF's four quoted bullets) — not the digest-binding Fixed Point.\n\nPlus one bookkeeping item: the outline declares `D2.2` exclusively, but §1/§7/§8 teach from snapshots tagged **D4**. That's under-declaration rather than scope creep, but D4 is 12% of the exam and this is one of few chapters teaching its ecosystem content — left uncorrected it risks a phantom \"D4 under-covered\" finding at book close.\n\n## Gap handling is a genuine strength\n\nSix of eight residual gaps handled. G-C, G-E and G-B are handled *better* than the manifest asked: the draft states what it cannot source in prose the reader sees, before making the argument, rather than only in a comment they don't.","ttft_ms":21967,"type":"result","duration_ms":384207,"uuid":"7ddb6be0-633b-4666-9e6a-5a8ae1e323e2","ttft_stream_ms":4331,"time_to_request_ms":228,"queued_turn_count":0}
